sub:assertion {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/approach/rule30-center-column--claude-opus-5-5--3> a prov:Plan, delphi:TestableHypothesis ;
    rdfs:label "Restricted-model lower bounds: the column is not generated by small automata" ;
    dct:subject <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/rule30-center-column> ;
    dct:description "Attack weak forms of problems 1 and 3 that are provable. Problem 3 asks for a lower bound on the effort to compute bit n, and unrestricted lower bounds are out of reach, but lower bounds against restricted models are finite questions. (a) For each base k in {2, 3, 4} and state count s ≤ 12, prove that no deterministic finite automaton with output (DFAO) reading n in base k reproduces the first N center bits. Encode as SAT (variables for transition and output tables; constraints from each prefix bit) and extract unsatisfiability certificates, which are DRAT proofs checkable by a verified checker. (b) Use Walnut to test whether the prefix is consistent with low-complexity morphic generation. (c) Prove in Lean, from (a) and the definitions, the theorem 'the center column is not k-automatic with ≤ s states'. That theorem implies, for example, that no constant-memory streaming algorithm over base-k digits of n computes the column with that much memory." ;

    delphi:proposedTest "Lean-checked theorems 'the Rule 30 center column is not generated by any base-k DFAO with at most s states' for stated (k, s). These are new and weak; their value is a precise, citable lower bound in a restricted model, and any satisfiable instance that extends to 10^9 bits would be a striking structural discovery." ;
    delphi:context "Fails if: SAT instances become intractable at small s (for example s ≤ 6), giving results too weak to matter; or proofs need native_decide-level computation that Lean cannot do in kernel." ;
    delphi:hypothesisType "approach/mixed" ;
    skos:note "Steps: 1. Generate SAT instances for (k, s) pairs over a prefix of N = 10^6 bits (smaller if unsatisfiability is reached earlier). 2. Solve with a DRAT-producing solver; check proofs with a verified checker. 3. Formalize the DFAO definition in Lean and state the non-automaticity theorems; import the certificate check by kernel-verified decoding of the minimal refuting prefix. 4. Record the largest (k, s) reached and the shortest refuting prefix for each." ;
    skos:note "Difficulty (proposer's estimate of completion within about a month of agent time): tractable" ;
    skos:note "Tool: sat-solver" ;
    skos:note "Tool: lean4" ;
    skos:note "Tool: automata-theory" ;
    skos:note "Tool: walnut" ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch02-05> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch12-27> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch06-14> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://datarepository.wolframcloud.com/resources/A-Billion-Bits-of-the-Center-Column-of-the-Rule-30-Cellular-Automaton> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://github.com/Walnut-Theorem-Prover/Walnut> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://www.rule30prize.org/> ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://github.com/fractastical/delphi/blob/ee98025a1960b300fcc06aa2106f51118b60f565/problems/approaches/claude-opus-5-5.json> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/rule30-center-column> a delphi:Concept, schema:Question ;
    rdfs:label "The Rule 30 center column" .

  <https://datarepository.wolframcloud.com/resources/A-Billion-Bits-of-the-Center-Column-of-the-Rule-30-Cellular-Automaton> dct:title "A Billion Bits of the Center Column of the Rule 30 Cellular Automaton" ;
    dct:description "Prefix used to generate SAT constraints" .
  <https://github.com/Walnut-Theorem-Prover/Walnut> dct:bibliographicCitation "Walnut, decision procedure for automatic sequences" .
  <https://www.rule30prize.org/> dct:bibliographicCitation "Prize problem 3 (computational irreducibility of the n-th cell)" .
}

